1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,874
An oblique path gives a longer distance between boundaries.

2
00:00:04,329 --> 00:00:08,171
Adjust the path to the local orientation of the boundaries.

3
00:00:08,632 --> 00:00:11,888
Preserve the measured path in images and records.

4
00:00:12,342 --> 00:00:15,500
An oblique path changes the measured distance.

5
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:20,736
Explain how the path relates to the intended direction of ring width.

6
00:00:21,539 --> 00:00:25,586
A known 10 mm spans 600 pixels in the image.

7
00:00:26,041 --> 00:00:31,489
Spatial scale: 600 divided by 10 = 60 px/mm.

8
00:00:31,502 --> 00:00:36,456
Width (mm) = boundary separation in pixels divided by 60.

9
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:40,587
Check that the scale applies to the image and transformation.

10
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:46,758
Converting pixels into length requires calibration under the relevant imaging conditions.

11
00:00:47,212 --> 00:00:49,732
Check the resulting units.

12
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:53,819
Seven boundary positions define six widths.

13
00:00:54,274 --> 00:00:58,518
Subtract successive positions, then apply the spatial scale.

14
00:00:58,971 --> 00:01:02,827
Positions increase from inner to outer wood in this example.

15
00:01:03,287 --> 00:01:07,379
A width is the difference between consecutive boundary positions.

16
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,128
Do not confuse the positions themselves with their differences.

17
00:01:12,931 --> 00:01:16,437
Scale: 600 px = 10 mm.

18
00:01:16,536 --> 00:01:19,944
Record each successive pixel difference and width in mm.

19
00:01:19,957 --> 00:01:23,557
Compare the sum of six widths with the endpoint distance.

20
00:01:24,012 --> 00:01:29,302
Pause to calculate the six differences and their units, and inspect whether any boundary

21
00:01:29,315 --> 00:01:31,668
position needs checking.

22
00:01:32,471 --> 00:01:36,721
600 divided by 10 = 60 px/mm.

23
00:01:36,918 --> 00:01:43,457
(670 − 100) divided by 60 = 9.50 mm.

24
00:01:44,381 --> 00:01:48,907
An endpoint check does not prove the boundaries were identified correctly.

25
00:01:49,368 --> 00:01:55,504
Compare boundary order and unit conversion as well as arithmetic when reviewing the answers.

26
00:01:56,307 --> 00:01:58,880
Units, precision and order.

27
00:01:59,336 --> 00:02:05,059
Check pixels versus mm, orientation, duplicate or missing rows and delimiters.

28
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,434
Suspicious values.

29
00:02:07,888 --> 00:02:12,947
Investigate extremes, jumps and repeated values in the original image.

30
00:02:13,401 --> 00:02:15,230
Reasons for revision.

31
00:02:15,691 --> 00:02:20,566
Recheck boundaries or transcription; preserve the change and its reason.

32
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:26,724
An unusual value calls for checking the original image, boundaries, injuries and units,

33
00:02:26,836 --> 00:02:29,396
rather than immediate deletion.

34
00:02:30,199 --> 00:02:32,746
Assign candidate calendar years.

35
00:02:33,201 --> 00:02:37,175
Match a dated reference and record overlap and mismatches.

36
00:02:37,635 --> 00:02:39,537
Return to the specimen.

37
00:02:39,991 --> 00:02:45,839
Investigate local absences, false rings, missed boundaries or measurement shifts.

38
00:02:46,293 --> 00:02:49,392
Separate observations and interpretation.

39
00:02:49,846 --> 00:02:54,484
Keep missing-position and dating hypotheses separate from observations.

40
00:02:54,938 --> 00:02:57,964
Measurement revisions can affect crossdating.

41
00:02:58,425 --> 00:03:01,968
Keep a record of which values changed and why.
